  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nA hot and humid day is ahead of us, one of many for the\nforeseeable. Looking at water vapor imagery, a shortwave trough\nover the central Rockies is beginning to approach the region. At\nthe surface, a quasi-stationary boundary is noted near the\nNebraska and South Dakota border. The remainder of this\nafternoon we can expect to see mostly sunny skies as temperatures\nclimb well into the 90s with dewpoints in the 60s and 70s. \nWidespread heat index values in the upper 90s and low 100s will \nbe a concern for the area. \n\nAs the aforementioned trough moves closer to the area this\nevening, storms are expected to develop over portions of north-\ncentral Nebraska and south-central South Dakota. A belt of \nenhanced mid-level winds will result in sufficient wind shear \nfor strong to severe storms. Large hail, damaging winds, and a \nlow chance of a tornado will all be possible. These storms are \nforecast to follow along the quasi-stationary boundary towards \nour area late this evening. As these storms approach, a gradual \nevolution into a complex of storms is anticipated. These storms\nare expected to move across portions of northeast Nebraska \nafter 8 PM, before exiting shortly after midnight. With the \ncomplex of storms, damaging winds is the primary hazard, but \nlarge hail and an isolated tornado remains possible. Showers and\nstorms may linger into early Wednesday morning, but these \nshould not pose a severe weather threat. With cloud cover over \nmuch of the area and south winds, expect temperatures to \nstruggle to cool into the 70s. \n\nThe heat slowly worsens on Wednesday. Temperatures are forecast\nto climb into the mid to upper 90s across most of the area. \nAcross east-central and southeast Nebraska, the combination of \nlimited relief from the heat at night, and afternoon heat index \nvalues in the low 100s is leading to cumulative heat stress. A \nHeat Advisory has been issued for these locations as a result of\nthis. The Heat Advisory runs into Thursday with similar \ntemperatures to Wednesday expected. The streak of overnight low \ntemperatures well into the 70s and high temperatures well into \nthe 90s closes out the week. With the heat not subsiding, an\nextension or expansion of the Heat Advisory is possible. Outside\nof the heat, there will continue to be a low chance (10-30%) of\nshowers and thunderstorms most nights, but this remains largely\nconfined to northeast Nebraska.\n\nThe heat continues into the weekend, but eases slightly. The\nworst of the heat could linger into Saturday, but Sunday onward\nsees a slow, but steady decrease in temperatures each day. Highs\nback in the 80s may be something to look forward to by Monday\nnext week. Continued nightly showers and thunderstorm chances\nremain during this time. Above-normal temperatures hold into\nnext week as the large-scale weather pattern remains stagnant.",
